---
title: "Linear cell basis"
output: rmarkdown::html_vignette
---

[![Experimental](https://img.shields.io/badge/lifecycle-experimental-orange.svg)](https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#experimental)

Needs a build made with `THIESSEN_EXPERIMENTAL=1 R CMD INSTALL r`; the
[experimental](experimental.html) page has the catalogue and the rule.

## Introduction

Under the published model each cell carries one value, so a
tessellation is a piecewise-constant function and a slope is a
staircase of cells. The linear cell basis gives each cell a local
linear function of the active coordinates, centred at the cell's
centre; the cell update draws the level and the slopes together from
their conjugate normal, and the structural moves integrate them out.
This is the Voronoi analogue of model trees in a BART ensemble (Prado,
Moral and Parnell 2021, MOTR-BART). Reach for it when the truth is
locally linear and rows are few: constant cells need many small cells
to follow a slope, linear cells need few large ones. At the default
ensemble size the two bases tie, because the prior scale the slopes
share with the levels falls with the size; the page shows where the
gain is and why it goes.

Prerequisite: BART (Chipman, George and McCulloch 2010) and AddiVortes
(Stone and Gosling 2025). The [model description](../model-description.html)
page has the notation.

## The model

    g(x; T, M) = mu_k + beta_k' (x_A - c_k)   for x in cell k
    (mu_k, beta_k) ~ N(0, sigma_mu^2 I_(d+1)),   sigma_mu = 0.5 / (k sqrt(m)),   k = 3

with x_A the active coordinates, c_k the cell's centre, so mu_k keeps
its role as the level at the centre, and m the number of
tessellations. The cell update draws (mu_k, beta_k) jointly from the
(d + 1)-dimensional conjugate normal, and the structural moves
integrate the coefficient vector out: per cell
-ln det(I + sigma_mu^2 A_k) / 2 + b_k' (sigma_mu^-2 I + A_k)^-1 b_k / 2,
with A_k and b_k the normal equations of the within-cell design
(1, x_A - c_k), the (d + 1)-dimensional form of the constant-basis
expression. The statement in full is the "Linear cell basis" section
of
[`docs/models.md`](https://github.com/l-thomson/thiessen/blob/dev/docs/models.md)
and the derivation note is
[`crates/thiessen/src/cells.rs`](https://github.com/l-thomson/thiessen/blob/dev/crates/thiessen/src/cells.rs).
Mean ensemble only, under hard membership, on min-max scaled columns.

## Example

The truth is a plane in five covariates with one oblique crease,
3 |x1 + x2 - 1| / sqrt(2) + x1 - 0.5 x2 + 1.5 x3 - x4 + 0.5 x5, observed
with noise of standard deviation 0.3 at 150 rows, with 500 held-out
rows and their noise-free values. A Voronoi boundary can lie along the
crease and an axis-aligned split cannot. The published rule, labelled
AddiVortes in the tables, and the linear basis are fitted at the
default ensemble size of 200 tessellations, on four chains, at the same
five seeds. The schedule is 1000 burn-in and 1000 kept sweeps per
chain, longer than the 300 and 300 the other pages in this group use.
Even so every fit warns: the largest R-hat over the four chains sits
between 1.01 and 1.03 for both bases, above the 1.01 the check asks
for, on the cell count, the residual scale or a training row. The
mixing table below puts that beside stochtree on the same quantity.

```{r setup}
source("_setup.R")
```

```{r example}
data <- creased_plane(n = 150, sd = 0.3, seed = 1)
schedule <- general_params(burn_in = 1000, draws = 1000)
constant <- thiessen_control(tessellations = 200, general_params = schedule)
linear <- thiessen_control(
  tessellations = 200, general_params = schedule,
  mean_params = term_params(cell = cell_params(basis = "linear"))
)
fits <- paired_fits(data, list(AddiVortes = constant, "linear cells" = linear), seeds)
summary(fits[["linear cells"]][[1]])
```

Three comparators fit a linear model inside each region of a
partition. stochtree (Herren and others 2026) is the BART
implementation with a regression in every leaf, `leaf_basis_train`;
it is fitted with 200 trees on the same schedule as four chains, one
run per chain seed on four cores, pooled as one fit, so it is treated
as the two bases are. MOTR-BART itself has no package and is cited as
the precedent. tgp (Gramacy 2007) fits the Bayesian treed linear model
of Chipman, George and McCulloch (2002), `btlm()`, one chain per seed
on the same schedule, and returns a normal predictive at the held-out
rows. Cubist (Quinlan 1992) fits rules with a linear model in each; it
has no chains and no predictive distribution, so it is fitted once and
scored on its error alone.

```{r comparators, message = FALSE}
rows <- rbind(data$train$x, data$test$x)
stochtree <- lapply(seeds, function(seed) {
  stochtree_chains(data, rows, seeds = 10 * seed + 1:4, num_trees = 200, burn_in = 1000, draws = 1000)
})
tgp <- lapply(seeds, function(seed) tgp_fit(data, data$test$x, seed, burn_in = 1000, draws = 1000))
cubist <- list(cubist_fit(data, data$test$x))
```

Every method is scored on the same 500 held-out rows through its
predictive distribution: the root mean squared error of the posterior
mean against the noise-free truth, the coverage and mean width of the
central 95 per cent predictive interval, the CRPS (lower is better)
and the log score (higher is better) of the held-out response, both
exact for the predictive mixture (Jordan, Krueger and Lerch 2019). The
mean over the five seeds with its standard error in parentheses:

```{r scores}
scores <- score(c(fits, list(stochtree = stochtree, tgp = tgp, Cubist = cubist)), data)
knitr::kable(score_table(scores))
```

The three sampled methods with draws of the mean function are also
compared on how they sample: the split R-hat and bulk effective sample
size (Vehtari and others 2021) of the mean function at the 150
training rows over the four chains, the wall-clock of the four chains
on four cores, and the smallest effective sample size per second. The
fit at seed 1 of each.

```{r mixing}
at_200 <- list(AddiVortes = fits$AddiVortes[[1]], "linear cells" = fits[["linear cells"]][[1]],
               stochtree = stochtree[[1]])
knitr::kable(mixing_table(at_200, data$train$x), digits = c(NA, 3, 3, 0, 0, 0, 1))
```

The two bases tie at this size, on every score, and both are ahead of
the three comparators on this truth: the tessellation ensembles put a
boundary along the crease, the tree methods approach it with a
staircase of axis-aligned splits, and Cubist's few rules leave most of
it unfitted. The linear basis costs about the same per sweep as the
constant one and mixes about as well. stochtree at 200 trees does not
mix in 1000 sweeps on five covariates and costs minutes where the
tessellations cost seconds.

## What it shows

Figure 1, the comparison: the error and the CRPS of every fit, one
point per seed, so the gaps between methods are read against the
spread over seeds. Cubist has no CRPS.

```{r comparison, fig.height = 3.5, fig.cap = "Figure 1. Held-out RMSE and CRPS of every fit at 200 tessellations or trees, one point per seed."}
ggplot(subset(scores, metric %in% c("rmse", "crps") & !is.na(value)), aes(model, value)) +
  geom_point(position = position_jitter(width = 0.1, height = 0, seed = 1)) +
  facet_wrap(~ metric, scales = "free_y") +
  labs(x = NULL, y = NULL)
```

Figure 2, the mechanism, at a size small enough that the cells are
visible: 5 tessellations, the same schedule, four chains, seed 1. The
transect along x1 with the other four covariates at 0.5 crosses the
crease at x1 = 0.5. The constant basis is a staircase; the linear
basis is a few pieces with the right slopes. Both fits warn, and the
section after the figure says why.

```{r small}
controls <- function(m) list(
  AddiVortes = thiessen_control(tessellations = m, general_params = schedule),
  "linear cells" = thiessen_control(tessellations = m, general_params = schedule,
    mean_params = term_params(cell = cell_params(basis = "linear")))
)
small <- lapply(paired_fits(data, controls(5), seeds = 1), `[[`, 1)
```

```{r mechanism, fig.height = 3.5, fig.cap = "Figure 2. The creased plane along x1, the other covariates at 0.5, at 5 tessellations: the truth and the posterior mean of each basis at seed 1."}
grid <- cbind(x1 = seq(0, 1, length.out = 200), x2 = 0.5, x3 = 0.5, x4 = 0.5, x5 = 0.5)
curves <- vapply(small, function(fit) colMeans(predictive(fit, grid)$mean), numeric(nrow(grid)))
transect <- data.frame(
  x1 = grid[, "x1"],
  method = factor(rep(c("truth", names(small)), each = nrow(grid)), c("truth", names(small))),
  f = c(creased_plane_f(grid), curves)
)
ggplot(transect, aes(x1, f, colour = method, linetype = method)) +
  geom_line() +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("dashed", "solid", "solid")) +
  labs(y = "f(x1, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5)", colour = NULL, linetype = NULL)
```

### Mixing at small ensembles

Small ensembles mix badly, for trees and tessellations alike: each
component carries a large share of the fit, so a structural move is
rarely accepted and a chain sits in one partition of the covariate
space. R-hat on the mean at a training row reports the step between
the partitions the four chains found. The same table as above for the
fits at 5:

```{r mixing_small}
knitr::kable(mixing_table(small, data$train$x), digits = c(NA, 3, 3, 0, 0, 0, 1))
```

Neither basis is at its posterior here, and the warnings say so; the
accuracy table rests on the fits at 200, where they are.

### The prior scale

The pure plane, the same truth without its crease, isolates the
slopes. At one tessellation the linear basis is a Voronoi partition
with a plane in each cell, and with one or two cells it is the least
squares fit under a normal prior, so its error should sit on the least
squares line, the oracle for this truth; it does. The error then rises
with the ensemble size to meet the constant basis. The prior scale is
the BART rule, sigma_mu = 0.5 / (k sqrt(m)), which keeps the sum of m
levels at the scale of the response; the slopes share it, so each
cell's slopes shrink towards zero by 1 / sqrt(m), and at 200 the
ensemble carries the plane in its levels, as the constant basis does.
The table carries the largest R-hat over the seeds at each size, which
is why the accuracy table is at 200.

```{r prior_scale, fig.height = 3.5, fig.cap = "Figure 3. Held-out RMSE on the pure plane against the ensemble size, the mean and standard error over five seeds; the dashed line is least squares."}
plane <- creased_plane(n = 150, sd = 0.3, seed = 1, f = "plane")
sizes <- c(1, 5, 20, 50, 200)
on_plane <- size_table(lapply(sizes, function(m) paired_fits(plane, controls(m), seeds)), sizes, plane)
ols <- lm(y ~ ., data.frame(plane$train$x, y = plane$train$y))
ols_rmse <- sqrt(mean((predict(ols, data.frame(plane$test$x)) - plane$test$f)^2))
knitr::kable(on_plane, digits = c(0, 0, 3, 4, 3, 1))
ggplot(on_plane, aes(tessellations, RMSE, colour = model)) +
  geom_line() +
  geom_pointrange(aes(ymin = RMSE - SE, ymax = RMSE + SE)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = ols_rmse, linetype = "dashed") +
  scale_x_log10(breaks = sizes) +
  labs(x = "tessellations", y = "held-out RMSE", colour = NULL)
```

### The boundary

The creased plane at the same sizes, five seeds each, the fits at 200
being the ones above. The linear basis is ahead at every size below
the default, most at one tessellation, where it uses under half the
cells of the constant basis; the gap closes by 200, where both are at
their posterior.

```{r boundary}
by_size <- c(lapply(sizes[-5], function(m) paired_fits(data, controls(m), seeds)), list(fits))
knitr::kable(size_table(by_size, sizes, data), digits = c(0, 0, 3, 4, 3, 1))
```

### Recovery with the sample size

The linear basis at 200 on the creased plane as the rows grow, five
seeds each: the error falls with n and the coverage of the 95 per cent
interval stays at its nominal level.

```{r recovery}
recovery <- do.call(rbind, lapply(c(150, 500, 1500), function(n) {
  larger <- creased_plane(n = n, sd = 0.3, seed = 1)
  at_n <- if (n == 150) fits["linear cells"] else paired_fits(larger, list("linear cells" = linear), seeds)
  cbind(n = n, score_table(score(at_n, larger))[c("RMSE", "coverage", "width")])
}))
knitr::kable(recovery)
```

## Validity

Validation is the calibration battery in the core's test suite; this
page is illustration. The linear basis carries known-answer tests of
the marginal likelihood and of the joint cell draw against quadrature,
simulation-based calibration and Geweke tests at two sizes, and a
broken-sampler fixture that drops the determinant term from the
integrated likelihood, which the gates reject. The figures are the
SBC rank-ECDF difference and the Geweke ECDF from the nightly run
named below.

```{r battery, results = "asis", echo = FALSE}
cat(readLines("figures/linear-cells/battery.md"), sep = "\n")
```

![SBC rank-ECDF difference](figures/linear-cells/sbc_ecdf_diff_linear.png)

![Geweke](figures/linear-cells/geweke_ecdf_linear.png)

## Limits

One prior scale serves the level and the slopes, so the slopes shrink
by 1 / sqrt(m) with the ensemble size and the basis changes nothing at
the default size; Prado, Moral and Parnell (2021) give the slopes a
prior of their own, and a slope scale that does not fall with m, with
its calibration under min-max scaling, is open. Nothing is said about
the identifiability of beta_k in a small or thin cell, or about the
interaction between the slope prior and the cell-count prior. There is
no weighted update under soft membership, and the variance ensemble
keeps the constant basis. No approximation or contraction result
exists for piecewise-linear Voronoi ensembles.

